PLAIN-SPEAK INTERPRETATION

The repair code was not broken.

The mechanical test was mixing up two different questions:

"Can the organism detect, repair, and recheck corrupted Task-2 work?"

and

"Does every corrupted job in this heavily loaded stress world still have
enough lifetime left to finish that repair?"

The first question is a mechanical implementation question.
It passes.

The second is a service-envelope question.
It remains deliberately strict for the real ten-world experiment.

We did not give requests more time,
change scheduling,
lower the load,
hide corruptions,
or weaken the primary rules.

The lab is now measuring the right thing at the right stage.

